verification/report.json
{
  "defects": [
    {
      "correction_required": "In external/lvgl/compose-16.py replace round(g[advanceWidth] * 16) with the pinned writer's semantics, math.floor(g[advanceWidth] * 16 + 0.5), then regenerate external/lvgl-16.png and refresh the output sha in external/lvgl-16.json. Do not touch the pinned lv_font_conv package.",
      "detail": "compose-16.py advances the pen with Python round(advanceWidth*16) while the pinned lv_font_conv writer (lib/writers/lvgl/lv_table_glyph.js:85) publishes adv_w = Math.round(advanceWidth*16). Python rounds half to even, JS rounds half up, so they differ exactly when the fractional part is .5 with an odd floor. Independently validated: of the 47 text codepoints only m (U+006D, advanceWidth 14.03125, x16 = 224.5 exactly) diverges, giving 224 in the composer versus 225 in the real LVGL table. font_info.json carries the same live doubles the writer sees (dump.js JSON-stringifies the font object), so the comparison is exact, not a float artifact. Effect on the shipped sample: 18 of 141 glyph placements sit 1px left of the true LVGL metric (all py <= js, max shift 1px), and recomposing with only the rounding changed alters 422 of 2787 ink pixels with total ink unchanged. My independent reimplementation of the composer reproduces the worker file pixel-for-pixel, confirming the diagnosis. The sidecar sentence claiming the composer uses the exact unit stored in adv_w is therefore incorrect for m.",
      "evidence": [
